WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:06.659
Technology is shrinking - smaller phones,
smaller laptops, smaller everything, but

00:00:06.669 --> 00:00:11.500
shrinking lenses, like those found in cameras and microscopes, has been a problem.

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:15.280
Material scientists are
trying to replace glass lenses with

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:21.700
something lighter and smaller based on
"meta surfaces" or engineered materials

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:29.580
In this meta-surface lens, tiny towers of titanium dioxide are arranged in a specific pattern to focus light.

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:32.900
Different patterns focus different colors of light.

00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:38.660
In a traditional microscope lens the glass varies in thickness between the middle and edges.

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:44.420
Inside the lens the wavefront of light is shaped via different thicknesses of glass.

00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:51.440
This focuses the light at one specific point beyond the lens. In the meta-surface lens, the transparent blocks

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:55.180
also bend the light toward the
focal point matching what happens in the

00:00:55.190 --> 00:01:01.460
glass lens, but with much less material.
The tiny meta-surface lens building blocks,

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:07.420
about six hundred nanometers in length, can achieve the same resolution and magnification

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:10.760
as a lens that is 5-6 centimeters in length.

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:16.300
Fabrications of these meta-lenses should also be much more cost-effective, compatible with silicon

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:21.480
chip technology versus complex polishing
and sometimes stringent requirements for

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:26.540
high-performance lenses. The next step is
to focus all of the visible spectrum

00:01:26.540 --> 00:01:31.660
into the same focal length using a meta-surface lens. Once this happens these

00:01:31.670 --> 00:01:36.840
glitter-sized lenses can replace the lens in
a smartphone, regular camera, microscope -

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:41.650
anywhere a lens might be needed and the
great shrinking can continue.

